system "BGN-PCGN:iu-Cans:Latn:2013" {

metadata {
  authority_id bgnpcgn
  id 2013
  language iso-639-2:iu
  source_script Cans
  destination_script Latn
  name Romanization of Inuktitut (2013 Agreement)
  url https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/permanent-committee-on-geographical-names
  creation_date 2013
  confirmation_date 2018-06
  description {
    The BGN/PCGN system for Inuktitut romanizes Canadian Aboriginal Syllabics
    (qaniujaaqpait) used for geographic names in Nunavut. The syllabic chart was
    approved by the Inuit Cultural Institute at Arviat in 1976. The BGN/PCGN
    agreement is dated 2013 and was checked for validity and accuracy in June 2018.
  }

  notes {
    note "Syllabics are official for geographic names only in Nunavut, Canada. The 1976 chart applies to all Inuktitut dialects."
    note "The s/h column is pronounced s in eastern dialects and h in the Kivalliq and Qitirmiut dialects. This map romanizes it as s."
    note "The ng column is a single consonant. It is romanized ng; Nattilingmiutut sources may write the velar nasal with the letter eng (U+014B)."
    note "The letters ł, š, and ř occur in Nattilingmiutut. The Roman encodings are U+0142, U+0161, and U+0159."
    note "Naaniit (finals) are superscript consonant forms and denote syllable-final consonants. A final followed by a consonant-initial syllable is the only occurrence of a consonant cluster in Inuktitut."
    note "A lengthened vowel is marked by a dot above the syllabic and is romanized as a doubled vowel. The map accepts the precomposed long-vowel characters."
    note "The barred-s column (header h, footnote 2) and the š column have no Unicode encoding in the source document; the source presents them as raster images. This map does not accept those characters."
    note "The ř column is stored in the source PDF at the Unicode TH-CREE codepoints U+15A7, U+15AA, U+15AC, and U+15AE. The map romanizes those codepoints as ři, řu, řa, and ř."
    note "The Roman letter H is inserted in syllabics for some words borrowed from English and is romanized h."
    note "The glottal stop is written with an inverted apostrophe (U+2018) in both syllabics and Roman script and is retained."
    note "Generally no more than two vowels appear in a row. The sequence uai occurs in the North Qikiqtani dialect."
    note "Consonants assimilate to the following consonant in pronunciation. The map romanizes the written final, so the written form of an assimilated sequence is preserved."
    note "The Roman-script columns are shown in lower case in the source. This map applies title case to each word."
  }
}

